    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Staining a deck is not difficult work, but it is unforgiving about sequence. Almost every failure traces back to one of two things: stain applied to wood that was not ready to accept it, or stain applied faster than it could be worked into the grain.
        </p>

        <div id="before-you-start" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Before You Start: Two Checks That Decide the Job
          </h2>

          <p>
            Do these before buying anything, because either can change the plan.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The water test.</strong> Sprinkle a little water on four or five spots around the deck — a sunny area, a shaded one, a high-traffic path, and somewhere under a chair. If the water beads and sits, something is repelling it: an old sealer still working, mill glaze on new lumber, or a factory finish. Nothing will penetrate that surface, so it needs stripping or more weathering first. If the water darkens the wood and disappears within a minute or two, the deck is open and ready.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The weather window.</strong> You need two dry days before you stain and, ideally, two after. Check the overnight lows as well as the daytime highs, because dew forms on cooling boards and behaves like light rain on a coating that has not cured. Most stains want temperatures somewhere between about 50 and 90 degrees Fahrenheit, and a night that drops below 50 can leave the surface tacky for days.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Whole Job in One Sentence</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Clean it, brighten it, let it dry properly, then stain two or three boards at a time from end to end and brush in everything you put down.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Every step below is an expansion of that sentence. If you remember nothing else, remember that the wood has to be genuinely dry and that you never stop in the middle of a board.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="tools" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What You Actually Need
          </h2>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Deck cleaner</strong> matched to the problem: a percarbonate cleaner for mildew and grey, a stripper if there is a failing coating to remove.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Wood brightener</strong>, usually oxalic acid based, to neutralise the cleaner and restore the wood's pH.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>A stiff synthetic-bristle deck brush</strong> on a pole, which does most of the cleaning work.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>A 4-inch or wider stain brush</strong> for back-brushing, plus a smaller one for railings and balusters.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>A stain pad or roller on a pole</strong> to lay stain down quickly across the field of the deck.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Drop cloths, painter's tape and a scraper</strong>, plus gloves and eye protection for the cleaning chemicals.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Enough stain</strong> — measure the square footage and compare it against the coverage rate on the can rather than guessing.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            On tool choice for application, the honest position is that the applicator matters far less than the back-brushing that follows it. Pads and rollers are fast, sprayers are faster still on railings and lattice, and all of them need a brush behind them. Our comparison of <a href="/brush-vs-roller-vs-sprayer-deck-stain/">brushes, rollers and sprayers</a> covers where each earns its place.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="step-clear" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Step One: Clear the Deck and Fix What Is Wrong
          </h2>

          <p>
            Take everything off — furniture, planters, grills, rugs. Anything left in place leaves an unstained rectangle you will look at for three years. Sweep thoroughly, then get a putty knife or a thin scraper down between the boards. The debris packed in those gaps holds water against the board edges and is a common reason decking rots from the sides inward.
          </p>

          <p>
            Now walk the deck and look at it properly. Drive down any nail heads or screws that have lifted, replace fasteners that are rusting through, and check for boards that are soft, split or cupped badly enough to hold water. Replacing a board is far easier before staining than after, and a new board can be stained along with everything else. Look at the joists and ledger while you are down there, especially where the deck meets the house.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protect what you cannot move. Tape plastic over adjacent siding, mask off the tops of any composite or metal railing components, and wet down the plants around the deck before applying cleaner, then rinse them again afterwards. Deck cleaners are generally not kind to foliage, and a soaked plant absorbs less of what lands on it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="step-clean" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Step Two: Clean the Deck
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cleaning is not about appearance. It is about removing everything that would sit between the stain and the wood: mildew, pollen, the loose grey surface fibres left by UV damage, and any remnant of an old finish. A deck can look clean and still be covered in a layer that stops penetration.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wet the boards first, then apply the cleaner and let it dwell for the time on the label — usually ten to fifteen minutes — without letting it dry out. Scrub with the stiff brush along the grain, working in manageable sections, then rinse thoroughly. On a badly weathered deck, a second pass on the worst areas is normal. Our guide to <a href="/best-deck-cleaner/">choosing a deck cleaner</a> covers which chemistry to pick for mildew, greying, or removing an old coating, because they are genuinely different products.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you use a pressure washer, use it with restraint. Stay under about 1,500 psi, fit a wide fan tip, keep it moving, and hold it well back from the surface. Softwood decking is easy to damage, and the fuzzy, furred surface a pressure washer leaves behind actually drinks stain unevenly and wears faster afterwards. It also drives a lot of water into the boards, which is why the drying time doubles. Most decks do not need one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="step-brighten" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Step Three: Brighten, Then Dry Properly
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most deck cleaners are alkaline, and they leave the wood surface alkaline too, which darkens some species and interferes with how stain takes. A brightener — typically oxalic acid — neutralises that, lifts tannin and iron stains around fasteners, and leaves the grain open. It takes twenty minutes and makes a visible difference, and skipping it is one of the most common reasons a well-cleaned deck still stains unevenly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then wait. Twenty-four hours is a minimum after a light wash; forty-eight or more after pressure washing, and longer again in humid or cool weather. Boards dry from the outside in, so a surface that looks and feels dry can still be wet a few millimetres down, and stain applied to it will not penetrate and may cloud. Use the water test again as the go/no-go: if it soaks in within a minute or two across the whole deck, you are ready.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sanding is optional and situational. It is worth doing on a deck that was pressure washed and left furry, on new smooth lumber with mill glaze, and on handrails where splinters matter. Use 60 to 80 grit and do not go finer, because a polished surface closes the grain and reduces absorption. Vacuum or blow the dust off afterwards. The full preparation sequence, including when stripping is unavoidable, is covered in <a href="/how-to-prep-a-deck-for-staining/">prepping a deck for staining</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="step-apply" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Step Four: Applying the Stain
          </h2>

          <p>
            Stir the stain thoroughly and keep stirring it as you go — the pigment settles, and a can that is not stirred halfway through the job produces a deck with two different colours on it. If you are using more than one can, box them: pour them all into a larger bucket and mix, so that any batch variation is spread across the whole deck rather than showing up as a line.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start with the railings, balusters and any vertical surfaces, then do the floor last so you are not leaning over finished work or dripping onto it. Plan your exit so you finish at the steps or a door rather than in a corner.
          </p>

          <p>
            The floor technique is the part that matters. Work on two or three boards at a time and run them from one end to the other without stopping. Lap marks form wherever fresh stain overlaps stain that has begun to set, so the only place a wet edge should ever meet a drier one is a natural board joint, where the eye reads it as part of the deck. Stopping halfway along a sixteen-foot board leaves a line you will see for years.
          </p>

          <p>
            Back-brush everything. Whatever tool laid the stain down, follow immediately with a brush worked along the grain, spreading the coating evenly and driving it into the wood. This is not an optional refinement: unbrushed stain sits on the surface in an uneven film, dries blotchy, stays tacky where it pooled, and wears off early. Pay particular attention to end grain at board ends and around cuts, which absorbs far more than face grain and is where water gets in.
          </p>

          <p>
            Apply only as much as the wood will take. Penetrating stains should be absorbed within about ten to fifteen minutes; anything still glossy and sitting on the surface after that is excess and should be brushed out or wiped off. Over-application is the leading cause of a deck that stays sticky, and our guide to <a href="/deck-stain-drying-time/">deck stain drying times</a> covers how to recognise and fix that.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="step-second-coat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Step Five: The Second Coat Question
          </h2>

          <p>
            The instinct to add a second coat for extra protection is usually wrong for penetrating stains. Wood absorbs a finite amount, and once it is saturated, anything more remains on the surface where it cannot cure properly. That surplus is what goes shiny in patches, stays tacky in warm weather, and peels the following spring.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rule that works is wet-on-wet: if a second coat is called for, apply it while the first is still absorbing, within the window the manufacturer specifies — often as little as thirty to sixty minutes. Applying a second coat the next day, onto a surface that has already sealed itself, is how people create the tacky film they were trying to avoid. Solid stains behave differently, being film formers, and generally do want two proper coats with the stated drying time between them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Vertical surfaces are the honest exception. Railings, balusters and fascia absorb less and shed more as you apply, so a second pass there is often genuinely useful even when the floor does not need one. The full decision, by stain type, is covered in <a href="/how-many-coats-of-deck-stain/">how many coats of deck stain you need</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="step-cure" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Step Six: Curing, and Moving the Furniture Back
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dry and cured are different states, and the gap between them is where good work gets ruined. Most deck stains are dry to the touch in a few hours and take light foot traffic after 24 to 48 hours, but full cure — the point at which the coating has hardened and is resistant to pressure and moisture — usually takes several days and can take longer in cool or humid conditions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Furniture is the thing to be patient about. A planter or a table foot on a coating that feels dry but has not hardened presses the finish into the wood and leaves a permanent mark, and plastic feet can bond to a soft surface and lift it when moved. Give it at least the manufacturer's stated cure time, and put felt or wide pads under anything heavy when it goes back.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rain within the first day or two is the other risk, and it is the reason for checking the overnight forecast rather than the daytime one. A shower on a stain that has not cured can leave water spots, dull patches or a washed-out area that needs recoating. If rain catches you out, let everything dry completely and assess in good light before deciding whether a touch-up is needed — many decks look far worse when wet than they turn out to be.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once cured, the maintenance is genuinely light: sweep it, keep the gaps between boards clear, wash it once a year, and watch the water test. When water stops beading and starts soaking in, the finish is asking to be renewed. That is covered in <a href="/how-often-to-stain-a-deck/">how often to stain a deck</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">A Realistic Weekend Schedule</h2>
        <p>
          The most useful thing you can do before starting is to stop thinking of this as a one-day job. On a typical deck the honest schedule runs across four days, and compressing it is where most failures begin. Friday evening is for clearing the deck, sweeping, cleaning out the gaps between boards and doing any repairs. That takes an hour or two and it means Saturday morning starts with a bare, sound surface.
        </p>
        <p>
          Saturday is cleaning and brightening. Wash in sections, scrub, rinse, apply the brightener, rinse again, and then stop — the temptation to keep going into a stain coat on the same day is exactly the mistake that produces a blotchy finish. Sunday is a waiting day, which feels like wasted time and is not. If it is humid, or you used a pressure washer, Sunday and Monday are both waiting days.
        </p>
        <p>
          Staining then takes most of one morning and early afternoon on an average deck, starting once the dew has burned off and finishing before the boards get hot in the late-afternoon sun. Working in the shade as it moves round the deck gives the best results, because stain that flashes off in direct sun never penetrates properly. Then the deck needs another day or two before furniture returns.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Staining Railings, Balusters and Stairs</h2>
        <p>
          Vertical work is slower per square foot than the deck floor and it is where most of the mess happens, which is why it goes first. Balusters are the worst of it: there is a lot of surface area, very little of it flat, and every one of them has four sides and two end cuts. A narrow brush and patience beat any clever tool here, though a sprayer with careful masking genuinely saves time on a long run of them.
        </p>
        <p>
          Handrails deserve extra attention because they are the part of the deck people touch. Sand them smooth before staining if there is any roughness, and take care to work stain into the underside and the ends, where water sits and where failures usually start. A rail that has been properly coated on all four faces lasts far longer than one done from above only.
        </p>
        <p>
          Stairs are worth doing last of all, working from the top down, so you always have somewhere dry to stand and a clear way off the deck. Treads take more traffic and more weather than anything else on the structure, so they will be the first thing to need attention at recoat time, and it is worth applying a full, well-brushed coat rather than a thin one. Do not forget the stair nosing and the end grain of each tread.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">If the Result Is Not What You Wanted</h2>
        <p>
          A blotchy finish almost always means the wood was not uniformly ready — either it was still damp in places, or an old finish was still active in others, or the boards had different absorption because some were new. If the blotching is mild, a light wash and a second thin coat once the deck is dry will often even it out. If it is severe, the honest fix is stripping and starting again, which is covered in <a href="/how-to-strip-deck-stain/">stripping deck stain</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          A sticky deck a week later means too much stain was applied, or it went on in unsuitable weather, or it was a second coat over a surface that had already sealed. Wipe the affected area with a rag dampened in mineral spirits for an oil-based stain, or the manufacturer's recommended cleaner for a water-based one, working the excess off rather than adding anything. Do this in a small test area first.
        </p>
        <p>
          Colour that is not what the sample suggested is the most common disappointment and the least fixable. Stain colour depends heavily on the species, the age and the absorbency of the wood, so the same product looks quite different on new pressure-treated pine, weathered cedar and dense hardwood. Testing on an offcut or an inconspicuous area before committing is the only reliable defence, and our <a href="/deck-stain-colors-guide/">deck stain colours guide</a> explains how the wood changes the result.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Staining Wood That Is Still Damp</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Boards dry from the outside in, so a surface that looks and feels dry can hold water a few millimetres down. Stain applied over it cannot penetrate, cures unevenly, and often clouds. Use the water test rather than the clock, and give a pressure-washed deck at least two clear days.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stopping in the Middle of a Board</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Wherever fresh stain meets stain that has started to set, a lap mark forms. Running each board from end to end confines those transitions to board joints, where they are invisible. Plan the work so a phone call or a refill never happens halfway down a long run.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Back-Brush</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rolling or spraying deposits stain on the surface rather than in the grain. Without a brush behind it the film dries unevenly, pools in low spots, stays tacky and wears off early. Back-brushing every square foot is the single highest-value habit in the whole job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying More Than the Wood Can Absorb</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A penetrating stain protects by soaking in, and once the wood is saturated the surplus sits on top where it never cures properly. That is what goes shiny, stays sticky and peels next spring. If it has not absorbed in ten to fifteen minutes, brush it out or wipe it off.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Working in Direct Sun on Hot Boards</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hot wood makes the carrier flash off before the stain can penetrate, leaving a thin surface layer and visible lap marks because the wet edge sets almost immediately. Follow the shade around the deck, and if the boards are too hot to rest a hand on, wait.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you stain a deck properly?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Clear and repair the deck, wash it with a wood cleaner, apply a brightener, let it dry two full days, then stain two or three boards at a time along their whole length, back-brushing everything you apply. Do not stop mid-board and do not apply more than the wood absorbs.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you have to pressure wash a deck before staining?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A deck cleaner and a stiff brush handle most decks, and a pressure washer used carelessly furs the surface fibres. If you use one, stay under about 1,500 psi with a wide fan tip held well back, and allow at least two dry days afterwards.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should a deck dry before staining?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At least 24 hours after a light wash and 48 or more after pressure washing, longer in humid or cool weather. Do not judge by appearance, because boards dry from the outside in. Sprinkle water on several areas: soaking in within a minute or two means ready.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What temperature should it be to stain a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most stains want roughly 50 to 90 degrees Fahrenheit with overnight lows staying above about 50. The bigger issue is direct sun on the boards, which flashes the stain off before it can penetrate. Work on surfaces that are not hot to the touch.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you avoid lap marks when staining a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Work two or three boards at a time and run each from end to end without stopping, so a wet edge only ever meets a drier one at a board joint. Lap marks form where fresh stain overlaps stain that has already begun to set.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should you back-brush deck stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, on every square foot, whatever tool put it down. Rolling and spraying leave stain on the surface; back-brushing works it into the grain and evens the film so nothing pools and goes sticky. Skipping it is the most common cause of a blotchy finish.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long after staining can you walk on a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually 24 to 48 hours for light foot traffic, and longer before furniture goes back, depending on the product, the humidity and how heavily it was applied. Dry to the touch is not cured — a planter on an uncured coating leaves a permanent ring.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you stain a deck in one day?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The staining itself is usually one day on an average deck, but the project is not. Cleaning and brightening take a day and drying takes one to two more. Compressing that by staining damp wood is the most common reason a job fails within a season.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

