        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Staining a deck is a four-day job with one day of staining in it. Clear and repair, clean, brighten, then wait until the wood genuinely accepts water before anything else happens. That waiting is not padding in the schedule; it is the step that decides whether the coating penetrates or sits on top and fails.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            On application, two habits carry the result. Run each board end to end so lap marks land at joints, and back-brush every square foot regardless of what laid the stain down. Apply only what the wood absorbs in ten to fifteen minutes, and brush out or wipe off anything still sitting on the surface after that.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then be patient again. Dry to the touch is not cured, and furniture returned too early leaves marks that outlast the coating. Give it the full cure time, put pads under anything heavy, and the job should hold for two to three years on a semi-transparent stain before the water test tells you it is time to do it again.
          </p>
        </div>


